# Server / client boundary (HARD)

Server-only code, meaning Cloudflare bindings (`cloudflare:workers` `env`:
D1, R2, secrets), password hashing, OAuth token exchange, Turnstile
verification, and any SQL, **must never end up in the browser bundle**. A
leak ships our DB schema,
auth flow, and attack surface to every visitor. This is not negotiable.

## The boundary

- Touch `env` / bindings / secrets **only inside** `createServerFn().handler()`
  or a route's `server.handlers`. Those are the only places guaranteed to run
  server-side and be stripped from the client.
- A module that does `import { env } from "cloudflare:workers"` is **server
  code**. It may be imported by server functions and server route handlers,
  never by a client component, the root shell, or rendered JSX.
- Across the boundary, share **types only**: `import type { SessionUser } from
  "entities/session"`, never a value import that drags the module in.
- Keep pure logic (parsing, hashing shape, cookie serialization) separate from
  the `env` I/O so the pure parts can be tested and the I/O stays at the edge
  (see `code-organization.mdc` → side-effect boundaries).

## How it stays out of the client

TanStack Start compiles `createServerFn().handler()` bodies and `server.handlers`
out of the client bundle (the client gets an RPC stub). `cloudflare:workers` is a
Worker-runtime virtual module, so the client build marks it `external` with
`treeshake.moduleSideEffects: "no-external"` (`vite.config.ts`). Because `env` is
read only inside those server-stripped bodies, the now-unused import tree-shakes
away: no `import "cloudflare:workers"` survives in a browser chunk.

So the safety comes from the boundary above (never read `env` or run
SQL/secrets/auth outside a server function), not from a bundler guard. Verify
after any change that touches the line: `bun run build`, then confirm `dist/client`
has zero `cloudflare:workers` references and no server markers (DB SQL, OAuth
endpoints, `pbkdf2`).

## The boundary cuts both ways: loaders run on the server

Route loaders execute on the server during document requests (a hard refresh
server-renders the route), so a loader that touches a browser-only API
(IndexedDB, localStorage, `window`, `navigator`) crashes exactly and only on
refresh, while every client-side navigation works. This class of bug has
shipped before: a loader that read client-local storage killed its screen on
reload. Any route whose loader needs client-local facts declares
`ssr: false` with a comment naming the API that forces it, and the loader
then runs only in the browser.
